we pray with me  spirit of the Living God come now and  grow our faith deepen our hope and  strengthen our love and inspire within  each one of us the desire to be your  faithful friends forever amen so three  feet of snow and three weeks later we  are picking up the Gospel reading again  right after the baptism of Jesus you may  remember how the heavens were opened and  the Spirit of God descended like a dove  and the voice proclaimed you are my son  the beloved in whom I am well pleased  it's been a couple weeks it might be  fuzzy but you remember right you're with  me so here comes Jesus just up from the  Jordan he is full of the Holy Spirit and  still ringing the river out of his  clothes when Luke reports that he is led  by the spirit into the wilderness for 40  days where he is tested by the adversary  I was reminded of that opening scene  from the book of job do you know it  where God is beaming and bragging over  job there's no one like him and all the  earth says God a blameless and upright  man who fears God and turns away from  evil God is gushing with pride and then  the Satan this sort of litigator of the  heavenly courts pipes up and volunteers  to try the strength of jobs jobs  fidelity almost on a kind of wager here  with no introduction this same character  shows up again this time to put Jesus on  trial and to see how he fares he kicks  off this incredibly witty and calculated  jousting match with Jesus so all of you  debaters and mock trailers will probably  want to take a few notes Jesus hasn't  eaten a thing for 40 days out in the  wilderness he's in the middle of the  desert and so this adversary is a  cunning devil  all he suggests very casually if you're  the Son of God why don't you command  that stone to become a loaf of bread  it's a pretty mildly reasonable request  it's also a challenge that cuts straight  into the heart of Jesus's identity and  his authority the heavens were parted  the Dove descended it was proclaimed you  are my son and so here the Satan starts  to probe very subtly into what exactly  that means you've been in the wilderness  for 40 days without food Moses led God's  people through the wilderness for 40  years and God provided water out of the  rock and bread from heaven since you are  the son of God you must be able to feed  the Sheep provide for all of the people  and their bodily needs command that  stone to become a loaf of bread be the  new Moses and lead your people into  freedom if you feed them they will  follow you Jesus replies it is written  one does not live by bread alone it's  rather Curt and dismissive but the devil  seems unfazed he just presses on he  drags Jesus up to the mountaintop where  they gaze down over all the kingdoms of  the world and Satan says worship me and  I will give you all their authority  David was the shepherd who became king  The Anointed One chosen by God David  defeated the giant without any armor at  all and United all of Israel around  Jerusalem God established his throne  forever Jerusalem is rightly yours and  Rome to in all of the kingdoms of the  world you can have them without armor I  will give them to you and you can be the  new David to unite Israel once more in  peace the people will love you and they  will fear you and they will follow you  Jesus replies it is written worship the  Lord your God and serve only him so  finally the devil drags him  to Jerusalem places him on the pinnacle  of the temple the central seat of the  religious authority for all of Israel  and says if you are the son of God throw  yourself down and here Satan gets a  little bit snarky for it is written God  will command the angels to protect you  they will bear you up and you will not  dash your foot against a stone if Jesus  wants to play with parables out of the  Bible then Satan can quote scripture as  well as the next guy and he starts  throwing it right back at him and says  the chief priests and the elders they  ruled the religious world of Israel they  narrate the story of God and the  people's place in it but you are the son  of God challenge the chief priests take  on the temple reveal your true identity  before all the people if you stun them  with signs and with miracles the people  will love you and they will fear you and  they will follow you and Jesus closes  with this last line it is said do not  put the Lord your God to the test and  suddenly the test is over this is the  kind of double entendre that makes  rhetoric geeks like me do a little dance  it reads just like a proverb just in  that same kind of short school lesson  format that all of his other responses  do but it functions like a command do  not put the Lord your God to the test  and it's done Jesus might as well have  said I am the lord your god do not put  me to the test we read this story over  and over traditionally as a source of  evil tempting Jesus to betray God in  order to gain power we here in Jesus's  words expressions of human faithfulness  and obedience to God but in fact in his  mouth they are God's words to us all  along hovering just behind Jesus's  responses is the heart  of Deuteronomy it's the core identity  and relationship of the people of God  Israel with God it follows just after  the Shema if you're familiar with it  it's that fundamental confession of  Jewish faith hear o Israel the Lord is  our God the Lord alone and so Jesus is  pulling from Deuteronomy just after the  Shema saying remember the long way the  Lord your God has led you these forty  years in the wilderness in order to  Humble you testing you to know what was  in your heart whether or not you would  keep his Commandments God humbled you by  letting you hunger and then by feeding  you with manna in order to make you  understand that one does not live by  bread alone but by every word that comes  from the mouth of God take care that you  do not forget the Lord who brought you  out of the house of Egypt out of the  house of slavery the lord your god you  shall fear him alone shall you serve do  not follow other gods any of the gods of  the people around you these are the  words that Moses was speaking to the  people of God just as they were on the  cusp of inheriting the promised land  they were getting ready to cross over  into the land that they had been waiting  for for 40 years and Moses is speaking  these words of wisdom and encouragement  and guidance before they go into that  place but here they're no longer Moses's  words to this ancient people in some  foreign place they are in the mouth of  Jesus God's words to us today suddenly  the tables are turned it is not Jesus  who is being put on trial to determine  his faithfulness but we are with all of  our misguided and short-sighted  expectations of who the son of God will  be Jesus is the Son of God and he is  bidding us follow me but we like to  follow other kinds of God's we follow  God's who promised to feed and entertain  us to keep us satisfied  consistently and deposit us comfortably  in a land flowing with milk and honey  where we will live happily ever after or  we prefer to follow God's who promised  to stop the violence and the chaos of  the world by any means necessary God's  who promised to unite us in a sort of  hand-holding peace and grant us the  safety and security that we want at the  cost of our own agency we preferred to  follow God's who will pronounce our  forgiveness and bless the status quo of  our lives without demanding anything  different from us or disrupting any of  the systems that benefit us but here  Jesus is reminding us repeatedly over  and over with these illusions that God  wants so much more for us in our lives  God has freed and feet fed God's people  in order to teach them how to live in  full communion with God with each other  with all of creation it's not about  self-satisfaction it's so that they can  learn to live as a humble and loving  community the promised land in  perpetuity is not actually about  defining borders and controlling the  security but that they might be a  blessing to the world they are invited  but not compelled to be there worship is  not about applying the rules to  determine who is in and who is out but  that other people might see their  community in Jubilee celebration and  long so desperately to be a part of it  and then to find delightfully that they  too are invited in Jesus invites us into  that covenant community that covenant  with God and with each other Jesus  invites us to leave behind all of our  shabby little shell self-made gods in  order to follow him but it's a way  without a clear map which makes some of  us nervous the nice thing is that we  have a clear leader Jesus leads us into  this kind of  risky business of trying to be God's  people in the world in the midst of  greed we are called to give up the will  to compete on the false terms of the  world and trust in God to provide in the  midst of violence we are called to give  up our instincts towards  self-preservation to be a beacon of  peace in the battle between religious  voices even we are called to give up our  need to be right in order to seek  instead to see clearly and to love the  stranger in our midst Jesus invites us  to follow not just for ourselves which  is our inclination with all of these  other gods but to follow instead for the  sake of God who loves us and saves us  even from ourselves Jesus goes on to  feed 5,000 in the desert to proclaim  God's kingdom in a way that threatens  all of the local authorities to  dramatically heal people and reinterpret  the Scriptures upsetting all of the  systems that the chief priests have held  in place the crowds would follow they  would love him and fear Him and they  would follow him but they were fickle  and I wonder if he knew even then that  the people would put the Lord their God  to the test opposing and scheming  against him and driving him all the way  to the cross

